 No implementation can promise that one person has only one account or that an application cannot be attacked. Unique emails, bot checks, quotas and throttling reduce abuse; availability against distributed attacks also depends on deployment infrastructure.
+
+## Local development tools
+
+The `/dev/inbox` endpoint exists only in Development and requires both a loopback remote IP and a localhost/loopback Host header. It does not trust forwarded headers. Responses disable caching and embedding, and file requests accept only generated message filenames. It exposes local confirmation/reset messages to local developers, so Development must never be publicly hosted or used with a production database. Protect the `.dev-mail` folder and delete old local messages when finished.
+
+`--create-demo` explicitly creates a confirmed development account with a new random password; it never seeds shared credentials at startup and is rejected in Production. The SMTP diagnostic is an explicit CLI command, not a public endpoint. Anonymous account responses remain generic to limit email enumeration.
